<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vectors-bisect-trisect">
<title>Geometry using vector calculations</title>

<introduction><p>
	For each pair of points <m> P,Q </m>:
	<ol>
		<li> Use a vector calculation to determine the point that bisects segment <m> \absegment{PQ} </m>. </li>
		<li> Use a vector calculation to determine the two points that trisect segment <m> \absegment{PQ} </m>. </li>
	</ol>
	<alert>Hint.</alert>
	Think of vector addition as a sequence of consecutive displacements,
	beginning at the origin.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> <m> P(1,-8) </m>, <m> Q(-7,-3) </m>. </p></statement>

	<solution><p>

		Let <m> \uvec{u} </m> represent the vector associated to directed line segment <m> \abray{OP} </m>
		and let <m> \uvec{v} </m> represent the vector associated to directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m>,
		so that <m> \uvec{u} = (1,-8) </m> and <m> \uvec{v} = (-8,5) </m>.

		<ol>

			<li>
			The bisecting point will be the terminal point of the vector
			<md> \uvec{b} = \uvec{u} + \tfrac{1}{2} \uvec{v} = ( -3, -11/2 ) </md>,
			when placed with its initial point at the origin.
			That is,
			the bisecting point is <m> R(-3,-11/2) </m>.
			</li>

			<li>
			The trisecting points will be the terminal points of the vectors
			<md><mrow>
				\uvec{t}_1 \amp = \uvec{u} + \tfrac{1}{3} \uvec{v} = ( - 5/3, -19/3 ) \text{,} \\
				\uvec{t}_2 \amp = \uvec{u} + \tfrac{2}{3} \uvec{v} = ( -13/3, -14/3 ) \text{,}
			</mrow></md>
			when placed with their initial points at the origin.
			That is,
			the trisecting points are <m> R(-5/3,-19/3) </m> and <m> S(-13/3,-14/3) </m>.
			</li>

		</ol>

	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> <m> P(4,0,6) </m>, <m> Q(-2,9,-1) </m>. </p></statement>

	<solution><p>

		Let <m> \uvec{u} </m> represent the vector associated to directed line segment <m> \abray{OP} </m>
		and let <m> \uvec{v} </m> represent the vector associated to directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m>,
		so that <m> \uvec{u} = (4,0,6) </m> and <m> \uvec{v} = (-6,9,-7) </m>.

		<ol>

			<li>
			The bisecting point will be the terminal point of the vector
			<md> \uvec{b} = \uvec{u} + \tfrac{1}{2} \uvec{v} = (1,9/2,5/2) </md>,
			when placed with its initial point at the origin.
			That is,
			the bisecting point is <m> R(1,9/2,5/2) </m>.
			</li>

			<li>
			The trisecting points will be the terminal points of the vectors
			<md><mrow>
				\uvec{t}_1 \amp = \uvec{u} + \tfrac{1}{3} \uvec{v} = (2,3,11/3) \text{,} \\
				\uvec{t}_2 \amp = \uvec{u} + \tfrac{2}{3} \uvec{v} = (0,6, 4/3) \text{,}
			</mrow></md>
			when placed with their initial points at the origin.
			That is,
			the trisecting points are <m> R(2,3,11/3) </m> and <m> S(0,6, 4/3) </m>.
			</li>

		</ol>

	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
